{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-88ddc3836132ce80", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tModel-Agnostic Meta-Learning Initialization for Distributed\n2\tMultichannel Active Noise Control\n3\t\n4\tXiaoyi Shen1\n5\t1\n6\t\n7\tarXiv:2607.29117v1 [eess.AS] 31 Jul 2026\n8\t\n9\tState Key Laboratory of Acoustics and Marine Information, Institute of Acoustics, Chinese Academy of Sciences,\n10\tBeijing, 100190, China\n11\t\n12\temail: shenxiaoyi@mail.ioa.ac.cn\n13\tJunwei Ji2 , Woon-Seng Gan2\n14\t2\n15\t\n16\tSchool of Electrical and Electronic Engineer, Nanyang Technological University, 639798, Singapore\n17\t\n18\tDongyuan Shi3\n19\t3\n20\t\n21\tCenter of Intelligent Acoustics and Immersive Communications, Northwestern Polytechnical University, Xi’an, 710072,\n22\tChina\n23\t\n24\tJun Yang1,4\n25\t4\n26\t\n27\tSchool of Electronic, Electrical and Communication Engineering, University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing,\n28\t100049, China\n29\t\n30\tABSTRACT\n31\tDistributed multichannel active noise control (DMCANC) has emerged as a scalable framework for\n32\tlarge-area noise reduction, where multiple nodes operate local single-channel ANC controllers and\n33\texchange essential information to achieve global control. A key limitation of existing DMCANC\n34\timplementations lies in their reliance on zero or random initialization, which leads to slow\n35\tconvergence of adaptive filters and restricts the efficiency of inter-node collaboration. To address this\n36\tissue, this paper introduces a model-agnostic meta-learning (MAML)–based initialization strategy\n37\tfor DMCANC. By aggregating heterogeneous acoustic characteristics across nodes—including\n38\tprimary and secondary paths—a MAML framework is trained to learn an initialization that\n39\tgeneralizes effectively across distributed ANC systems. The MAML initialization is then deployed to\n40\tall nodes to improve convergence speed under both stationary and time-varying noise conditions.\n41\tNumerical simulations applied on broadband and real-world noise demonstrate that the proposed\n42\talgorithms achieves substantially faster convergence and improved noise reduction performance\n43\tcompared with conventional DMCANC, highlighting the potential of MAML initialization as an\n44\teffective method for large-scale ANC.\n45\tKeywords: Active noise control (ANC), Distributed multichannel ANC, model-agnostic meta-learning\n46\t(MAML)\n47\t\n48\t\fICSV32, Annual Congress of International Institute of Acoustics and Vibration (IIAV), 5 - 10 July 2026\n49\t\n50\t1.\n51\t\n52\tINTRODUCTION\n53\t\n54\tActive noise control (ANC) has been widely used as an effective technique for attenuating lowfrequency noise by generating anti-noise signals with the same amplitude and opposite phase [1–3].\n55\tBenefiting from adaptive filtering algorithms such as the filtered-x least mean square (FxLMS) [4–6],\n56\tANC systems are capable of operating in dynamic environments. With the increasing demand for\n57\tlarge-area noise reduction, multichannel ANC (MCANC) systems have been extensively studied [7,\n58\t8]. Conventional MCANC systems are typically implemented in a centralized manner, where a single\n59\tcontroller processes all sensor and actuator signals [9, 10]. Although such centralized schemes can\n60\tachieve optimal performance, they suffer from high computational complexity and limited scalability.\n61\tTo address these issues, distributed multichannel ANC (DMCANC) systems have been proposed [11,\n62\t12], in which multiple nodes independently perform local adaptation while exchanging information to\n63\tachieve global noise reduction. This distributed architecture provides improved scalability, flexibility,\n64\tand suitability for wireless implementation [13, 14].\n65\tRecent advances in DMCANC have focused on improving system robustness and practical\n66\tfeasibility under non-ideal communication conditions [15–17]. The mixed-gradient FxLMS\n67\t(MGDFxLMS) algorithm applied compensation filters to combine the received gradients and\n68\tmitigate crosstalk effects between each node [18]. MGDFxLMS has been proved haveing equivalent\n69\tnoise reduction performance to the centralized ANC system under ideal communication conditions.\n70\tFurthermore, intermittent communication strategies have been introduced to DMCANC (ICDMCANC) to reduce communication burden and computational complexity, while maintaining\n71\tperformance close to centralized systems [19]. In such frameworks, each node typically runs a local\n72\tadaptive algorithm, and global coordination is achieved through periodic information exchange.\n73\tDespite these improvements, most existing DMCANC methods rely on zero or random initialization\n74\tof control filters [20, 21].\n75\tHowever, the initialization of adaptive filters plays a critical role in determining the convergence\n76\tbehavior of ANC systems [22–24]. In distributed settings, poor initialization not only slows down\n77\tlocal adaptation but also limits the effectiveness of inter-node collaboration, especially under\n78\ttime-varying noise conditions [25]. This issue becomes more pronounced in large-scale DMCANC\n79\tsystems, where heterogeneous acoustic paths and dynamic environments further complicate the\n80\tconvergence process [26].\n81\tTo address this limitation, we propose a model-agnostic meta-learning (MAML)-based\n82\tinitialization framework for IC-DMCANC [27–30]. Unlike conventional approaches that treat\n83\teach ANC node independently, the proposed method leverages meta-learning to extract shared\n84\tknowledge from heterogeneous acoustic environments, including variations in primary and\n85\tsecondary paths. By training across multiple tasks, the MAML framework learns a generalized\n86\tinitialization that enables rapid adaptation when deployed in unseen DMCANC scenarios. The\n87\tproposed method is designed to be compatible with existing distributed ANC architectures. The\n88\tlearned initialization can be directly applied to each node as the initial control filter of the adaptive\n89\talgorithms, thereby significantly accelerating convergence without altering the underlying control\n90\tstructure. Extensive simulations under broadband and real-recorded noise conditions demonstrate\n91\tthat the proposed approach achieves faster convergence and improved noise reduction performance\n92\tcompared with conventional DMCANC methods.\n93\tThe remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section 2 introduces the DMCANC system\n94\tand reviews relevant adaptive algorithms and presents the proposed MAML-based initialization\n95\tframework. Section 3 provides performance analysis, followed by numerical simulations. Finally,\n96\tconclusions are drawn in Section 4 .\n97\t\n98\t2|11\n99\t\n100\t\fICSV32, Annual Congress of International Institute of Acoustics and Vibration (IIAV), 5 - 10 July 2026\n101\t\n102\tFigure 1: The block diagram of implemetation for IC-DMCANC.\n103\t2.\n104\t\n105\tPROPOSED METHOD\n106\t\n107\tIn order to imrove the convergence speed of IC-DMCANC, MAML is applied to pretrain the\n108\toptimal initial control filter for each node of IC-DMCANC, which is shown in Fig.1.\n109\t2.1. Distributed Multichannel ANC with Intermittent Communication\n110\tDMCANC is composed of K nodes, in the k-th node, the control signal can be calculated as:\n111\tyk (n) = wTk (n)x(n),\n112\t\n113\tk = 1, 2, ..., K,\n114\t\n115\t(1)\n116\t\n117\twhere yk (n) denotes the control signal of the k-th node, and x(n) is the reference signal captured by\n118\tthe reference sensor. wk (n) = [wk,1 (n), wk,2 (n), · · · , wk,L (n)]T represents the weights of adaptive filter\n119\tin the k-th node with the length of L, which is updated by steepest gradient descent method:\n120\t\u0002\n121\t\u0003\n122\tek − wk (n) .\n123\twk (n + 1) = wk (n) + µk x′kk (n)ek (n) + µk α w\n124\t(2)\n125\twhere µk and α denote the step size and penalty factor for the weight update equation of k-th node.\n126\tek stands for the center point of the range to which the control filter is restricted. ek (n) is the error\n127\tw\n128\tsignal of the k-th node and x′kk (n) is the filtered reference signal calculated by\n129\tx′kk (n) = ŝkk (n) ∗ x(n),\n130\t\n131\t(3)\n132\t\n133\twhere ŝkk (n) denotes the estimated self-secondary path skk (n) with length L s . The compensation filters\n134\tare introduced to make up for the difference between the node’s self-secondary path and inter-node\n135\t3|11\n136\t\n137\t\fICSV32, Annual Congress of International Institute of Acoustics and Vibration (IIAV), 5 - 10 July 2026\n138\t\n139\tcross-secondary path. The compensation filters cmk (n) can be estimated offline prior to the operation\n140\tof the ANC system:\n141\tcmk (n + 1) = cmk (n) + µc v′ (n)em (n),\n142\t(4)\n143\twhere v′ (n) denotes to the filtered WGN vector and em (n) is given by\n144\tem (n) = v′k (n) − v′m (n),\n145\tand v′k (n) and v′m (n) is generated by\n146\t\n147\t\n148\t\n149\tv′k (n) =\n150\t\n151\t\n152\tv′m (n) =\n153\t\n154\t(5)\n155\t\n156\tv(n) ∗ smk (n),\n157\tcTmk (n)v(n) ∗ smm (n),\n158\t\n159\t(6)\n160\t\n161\tin which v(n) is white Gaussian noise and smk (n) is cross-secondary path.\n162\tIntermittent communication (IC) strategy exchange data only at non-continuous, scheduled\n163\tintervals rather than at every sampling point:\n164\twk (n + 1) = wk (n − N + 1) +\n165\t\n166\tN−1\n167\tX\n168\t\n169\tµk x′kk (n − t)ek (n − t) +\n170\t\n171\tN−1\n172\tX\n173\t\n174\t\u0002\n175\t\u0003\n176\tek − wk (n − t) ,\n177\tµk α w\n178\t\n179\t(7)\n180\t\n181\tt=0\n182\t\n183\tt=0\n184\t\n185\twhich accumulates the algorithm’s gradient information over the N steps. The new central control\n186\tfilter can be approximated as\n187\te(r+1)\n188\te(r)\n189\tw\n190\t≈w\n191\tk\n192\tk + ϕk (n) +\n193\t\n194\tK\n195\tX\n196\t\n197\tϕm (n) ∗ cmk (n),\n198\t\n199\t(8)\n200\t\n201\tm=1\n202\tm,k\n203\t\n204\twhere\n205\te(r)\n206\tϕk (n) = wk (n + 1) − w\n207\tk .\n208\t\n209\t(9)\n210\t\n211\t2.2. Model-Agnostic Meta-Learning Initialization\n212\tIn order to accelerate the noise reduction process, we applied Model-Agnostic Meta-Learning\n213\t(MAML) initialization to obtain the optimal initial coefficients wok for k-th ANC node. The dataset\n214\tis collected seperately using primary path and seconadry path for this node. Here we use one\n215\tnode as example. The dataset used for k-th node coefficient initialization consists of randomly\n216\t(2)\n217\tsampled disturbance signals {d(1)\n218\tk (n), dk (n), · · · } and their corresponding filtered reference signal\n219\ttracks {x′kk (1) (n), x′kk (2) (n), · · · } in different ANC configuration settings. The error signal of the j-th\n220\twithin-task training e⋆k ( j) (n) is obtained from\n221\te⋆k (n) = dk( j) (n) − [x′ (kkj) ]T (n)ϱ(kj) ,\n222\t( j)\n223\t\n224\t(10)\n225\t\n226\twhere ϱ(kj) represents the j-th initial value of the control filter. It is presumed that the best initial\n227\tcontrol filter requires only a single update to reach the optimal solution wok :\n228\twok ( j) = ϱ(kj) − βe⋆k (n)x′kk ( j) (n),\n229\t( j)\n230\t\n231\tin which β denotes the step size for the updation process in the within-task training.\n232\tIn j-th within-task testing, the testing data pair is given by\n233\t ( j)\n234\t†\n235\t\n236\t\n237\t= dk ( j) (n),\n238\tdk (m)\n239\th\n240\ti\n241\t\n242\t\n243\tx† ( j) (m − i) = x′ ( j) (n − i) x′ ( j) (n − i − 1) · · · 01×i T ,\n244\tkk\n245\t\n246\tkk\n247\t\n248\t(11)\n249\t\n250\t(12)\n251\t\n252\tkk\n253\t\n254\t4|11\n255\t\n256\t\fICSV32, Annual Congress of International Institute of Acoustics and Vibration (IIAV), 5 - 10 July 2026\n257\t( j)\n258\t\n259\twhere i = 1, 2, · · · , L, and the j-th within-task testing error e†k (m) is obtained from\n260\t( j)\n261\t\n262\t( j)\n263\t\n264\t( j)\n265\t\n266\te†k (m − i) = dk† (m − i) − [x†kk (m − i)]T wok ( j) .\n267\t\n268\t(13)\n269\t\n270\tAccording to the gradient descent method, the recursive formula ϱk is derived as\n271\tϱ(kj+1) = ϱ(kj) + ϵ\n272\t\n273\tL−1\n274\tX\n275\t\n276\t( j)\n277\t\n278\t( j)\n279\t\n280\tλi e†k (m − i)x†kk (m − i),\n281\t\n282\t(14)\n283\t\n284\ti=0\n285\t\n286\twhere λ ∈ [0, 1] stands for the forgetting factor and ϵ ∈ (0, 1) denotes the learning rate. After withintask training and testing, the optimal initial value wok obtained in Equation 11 is applued for k-th node\n287\tas the initial coefficients to further update wk (n) .\n288\t3.\n289\t\n290\tSIMULATION RESULTS\n291\t\n292\tSimulations were conducted to validate the noise reduction performance of the proposed MAMLICDMCANC framework, implemented with six nodes as shown in Fig. 1. The primary and secondary\n293\tpaths were measured in a real acoustic environment using an acoustic chamber. The impulse response\n294\tof the secondary path for ANC node 1 is illustrated in Fig. 2. The lengths of the secondary path,\n295\tcompensation filter, and control filter were set to 256, 33, and 512 taps, respectively. The sampling\n296\tfrequency for all algorithms was set at 16 kHz. For comparison, centralized implementations and\n297\tMGDFxLMS, as well as the IC-DMCANC algorithm, were also evaluated.\n298\tIn the MAML initialization stage, broadband noise signals with frequency ranges of 100–1200 Hz,\n299\t800–1500 Hz, and 1200–2000 Hz were used to train the optimal initial coefficients. The training data\n300\tpairs were generated by passing the signals through the primary and secondary paths. The dataset\n301\twas split into 70% for training and 30% for validation. The learned initialization was then applied to\n302\tthe adaptive filters under different noise conditions, while it was initialized as a zero vector for other\n303\tcompared algorithms.\n304\t3.1. Tonal noise cancellation\n305\tA tonal noise consisting of 315 Hz and 500 Hz components was used for evaluation. The step size\n306\tfor all algorithms was set to 1 × 10−8 . The mean square error (MSE) curves of the six nodes are shown\n307\tin Fig. 3. It can be observed that the proposed method achieves the fastest convergence across all\n308\tnodes. IC-DMCANC and centralized exhibit comparable convergence speed, both outperforming\n309\tMGDFxLMS.The superior convergence performance of the proposed method is attributed to the\n310\tMAML-based initialization, which captures prior acoustic characteristics before online adaptation.\n311\tDue to differences in local acoustic environments, the steady-state performance varies slightly across\n312\tnodes; however, consistent convergence acceleration is observed.\n313\tFig. 4 presents the MSE and power spectrum of the error signals. The power spectrum was\n314\tcomputed over the final 3 seconds of the control process. As shown in Fig. 4(b), all methods\n315\tachieve comparable steady-state attenuation, while Fig. 4(a) demonstrates the faster convergence of\n316\tthe proposed method.\n317\t3.2. Broadband noise cancellation\n318\tBroadband noise with the frequency range of 200–800 Hz was used for evaluation. The step size\n319\twas set to 3 × 10−7 for all algorithms. The MSE curves for each node are shown in Fig. 5. Similar to\n320\tthe tonal noise case, the proposed method achieves the fastest convergence, while the other methods\n321\texhibit comparable convergence speed and similar steady-state performance.\n322\tFig. 6 shows the convergence behavior and power spectrum for ANC node 1. All algorithms\n323\teffectively attenuate noise within the target frequency band.\n324\t5|11\n325\t\n326\t\fICSV32, Annual Congress of International Institute of Acoustics and Vibration (IIAV), 5 - 10 July 2026\n327\t\n328\tFigure 2: Impluse response of estimated secondary path for ANC node 1.\n329\t\n330\tFigure 3: Mean square error of different algorithms for tonal noise with each ANC node: (a). Mean\n331\tsquare error in ANC node 1. (b). Mean square error in ANC node 2. (c). Mean square error in ANC\n332\tnode 3. (d). Mean square error in ANC node 4. (e). Mean square error in ANC node 5. (f). Mean\n333\tsquare error in ANC node 6.\n334\t3.3. Real noise cancellation\n335\tIn this simulation, a real recorded compressor noise was used. The step size settings were\n336\tconsistent with the previous simulations. The results indicate that the proposed method achieves the\n337\t6|11\n338\t\n339\t\fICSV32, Annual Congress of International Institute of Acoustics and Vibration (IIAV), 5 - 10 July 2026\n340\t\n341\tFigure 4: Convergence performance of different algorithms for tonal noise with ANC node 1:\n342\t(a). Mean square error of different algorithms. (b). Power spectrum of error signals of different\n343\talgorithms.\n344\t\n345\tFigure 5: Mean square error of different algorithms for braobdand noise (200 − 800Hz) with each\n346\tANC node: (a). Mean square error in ANC node 1. (b). Mean square error in ANC node 2. (c). Mean\n347\tsquare error in ANC node 3. (d). Mean square error in ANC node 4. (e). Mean square error in ANC\n348\tnode 5. (f). Mean square error in ANC node 6.\n349\tfastest convergence among all the compared methods.\n350\tFig. 7 and Fig. 8 illustrate the MSE and power spectrum of the error signals, respectively. As\n351\tshown in Fig. 7, the proposed method suppresses the noise from the early stage of the control process,\n352\twhereas other methods begin to achieve noticeable attenuation after approximately 10 seconds. Fig. 8\n353\t7|11\n354\t\n355\t\fICSV32, Annual Congress of International Institute of Acoustics and Vibration (IIAV), 5 - 10 July 2026\n356\t\n357\tFigure 6: Convergence performance of different algorithms for braobdand noise (200 − 800Hz) with\n358\tANC node 1: (a). Mean square error of different algorithms. (b). Power spectrum of error signals of\n359\tdifferent algorithms.\n360\tfurther confirms that the proposed method effectively attenuates noise within the target frequency\n361\trange.\n362\t\n363\tFigure 7: Mean square error of different algorithms for real recorded noise with each ANC node: (a).\n364\tMean square error in ANC node 1. (b). Mean square error in ANC node 2. (c). Mean square error\n365\tin ANC node 3. (d). Mean square error in ANC node 4. (e). Mean square error in ANC node 5. (f).\n366\tMean square error in ANC node 6.\n367\t\n368\t8|11\n369\t\n370\t\fICSV32, Annual Congress of International Institute of Acoustics and Vibration (IIAV), 5 - 10 July 2026\n371\t\n372\tFigure 8: Convergence performance of different algorithms real recorded noise with ANC node 1:\n373\t(a). Mean square error of different algorithms. (b). Power spectrum of error signals of different\n374\talgorithms.\n375\t4.\n376\t\n377\tCONCLUSION\n378\t\n379\tThis paper proposed a model-agnostic meta-learning (MAML)-based initialization framework for\n380\tdistributed multichannel active noise control with intermittent communication (IC-DMCANC). By\n381\tleveraging heterogeneous acoustic information across different nodes and environments, the proposed\n382\tmethod learned a generalized initialization that significantly improved the convergence behavior of\n383\tadaptive control filters. Unlike conventional DMCANC approaches that relied on zero or random\n384\tinitializations, the proposed framework enabled each node to start from a more informative initial\n385\tpoint, thereby accelerating adaptation and enhancing inter-node collaboration. The method was\n386\tfully compatible with existing distributed ANC architectures and could be seamlessly integrated into\n387\tpractical systems without modifying the underlying control structure. Simulations under different\n388\tnoise conditions demonstrated that the proposed method achieved faster convergence performance\n389\tcompared with conventional methods. These findings highlighted the importance of initialization\n390\tin DMCANC and validated the effectiveness of MAML in addressing this previously overlooked\n391\tproblem.\n392\tACKNOWLEDGEMENTS\n393\tThis research is supported by the Chinese Academy of Sciences Talent Introduction Program for\n394\tYoung Researchers (Grant Number: 552025000180).\n395\tREFERENCES\n396\t[1] Sen M Kuo and Dennis R Morgan. Active noise control: a tutorial review. Proceedings of the\n397\tIEEE, 87(6):943–973, 1999.\n398\t[2] Colin N Hansen. Understanding active noise cancellation. CRC Press, 1999.\n399\t\n400\t9|11\n401\t\n402\t\fICSV32, Annual Congress of International Institute of Acoustics and Vibration (IIAV), 5 - 10 July 2026\n403\t\n404\t[3] S. J. Elliott. Signal processing for active control. Academic, San Diego, Calif., 2001. Includes\n405\tbibliographical references and index.\n406\t[4] Simon S Haykin. Adaptive filter theory. Pearson Education India, 2002.\n407\t[5] Chaung Shi and Yoshinobu Kajikawa. A partial-update minimax algorithm for practical\n408\timplementation of multi-channel feedforward active noise control. In 2018 16th International\n409\tWorkshop on Acoustic Signal Enhancement (IWAENC), pages 1–15. IEEE, 2018.\n410\t[6] Bhan Lam, Chuang Shi, Dongyuan Shi, and Woon-Seng Gan. Active control of sound through\n411\tfull-sized open windows. Building and Environment, 141:16–27, 2018.\n412\t[7] Cheng-Yuan Chang, Xiu-Wei Liu, Sen M Kuo, et al. Active noise control for centrifugal and\n413\taxial fans. Noise Control Engineering Journal, 68(6):490–500, 2020.\n414\t[8] Xiaoyi Shen, Yu Guo, Junwei Ji, Dongyuan Shi, and Woon-Seng Gan. Multi-channel adjoint\n415\tleast mean square algorithm with momentum factor applied on active noise control. Mechanical\n416\tSystems and Signal Processing, 228:112415, 2025.\n417\t[9] Xiaoyi Shen, Dongyuan Shi, Zhengding Luo, Junwei Ji, and Woon-Seng Gan. A momentum\n418\ttwo-gradient direction algorithm with variable step size applied to solve practical output\n419\tconstraint issue for active noise control. In ICASSP 2023-2023 IEEE International Conference\n420\ton Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), pages 1–5. IEEE, 2023.\n421\t[10] Woon-Seng Gan, Dongyuan Shi, and Xiaoyi Shen. Practical active noise control: Restriction of\n422\tmaximum output power. arXiv preprint arXiv:2307.10913, 2023.\n423\t[11] Junwei Ji, Dongyuan Shi, Zhengding Luo, Xiaoyi Shen, and Woon-Seng Gan. A practical\n424\tdistributed active noise control algorithm overcoming communication restrictions. In 2023 Proc.\n425\t- ICASSP IEEE Int. Conf. Acoust. Speech Signal Process., pages 1–5. IEEE, 2023.\n426\t[12] Tianyou Li, Siyuan Lian, Sipei Zhao, Jing Lu, and Ian S. Burnett. Distributed active noise\n427\tcontrol based on an augmented diffusion fxlms algorithm. IEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio,\n428\tSpeech, and Language Processing, pages 1–15, 2023.\n429\t[13] Xiaoyi Shen, Woon-Seng Gan, and Dongyuan Shi. Multi-channel wireless hybrid active noise\n430\tcontrol with fixed-adaptive control selection. Journal of Sound and Vibration, 541:117300,\n431\t2022.\n432\t[14] Xiaoyi Shen, Dongyuan Shi, Zhengding Luo, Junwei Ji, and Woon-Seng Gan. A survey\n433\tof integrating wireless technology into active noise control. In INTER-NOISE and NOISECON Congress and Conference Proceedings, volume 270, pages 1624–1637. Institute of Noise\n434\tControl Engineering, 2024.\n435\t[15] Y. J. Chu, C. M. Mak, Y. Zhao, S. C. Chan, and M. Wu. Performance analysis of a diffusion\n436\tcontrol method for anc systems and the network design. Journal of Sound and Vibration,\n437\t475:115273, 2020.\n438\t[16] Tianyou Li, Li Rao, Sipei Zhao, Hongji Duan, Jing Lu, and Ian S. Burnett. An augmented\n439\tdiffusion algorithm with bidirectional communication for a distributed active noise control\n440\tsystem. The Journal of the Acoustical Society of America, 154(6):3568–3579, 2023.\n441\t[17] Yang Zhou, Haiquan Zhao, Dongxu Liu, and Xinnian Guo. Distributed active noise control\n442\trobust to impulsive interference. IEEE Sensors Journal, 2025.\n443\t[18] Junwei Ji, Dongyuan Shi, and Woon-Seng Gan. Mixed-gradients distributed filtered reference\n444\tleast mean square algorithm – a robust distributed multichannel active noise control algorithm.\n445\tIEEE Transactions on Audio, Speech and Language Processing, 33:1563–1575, 2025.\n446\t[19] Junwei Ji, Dongyuan Shi, Xiaoyi Shen, Woon-Seng Gan, Jie Chen, and Jun Yang.\n447\tImplementation of distributed multichannel active noise control with intermittent\n448\tcommunication and coprocessor assisted data combination.\n449\tMechanical Systems and\n450\tSignal Processing, 248:114024, 2026.\n451\t10|11\n452\t\n453\t\fICSV32, Annual Congress of International Institute of Acoustics and Vibration (IIAV), 5 - 10 July 2026\n454\t\n455\t[20] Dongyuan Shi, Bhan Lam, Kenneth Ooi, Xiaoyi Shen, and Woon-Seng Gan. Selective\n456\tfixed-filter active noise control based on convolutional neural network. Signal Processing,\n457\t190:108317, 2022.\n458\t[21] Dongyuan Shi, Woon-Seng Gan, Bhan Lam, Zhengding Luo, and Xiaoyi Shen. Transferable\n459\tlatent of cnn-based selective fixed-filter active noise control. IEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio,\n460\tSpeech, and Language Processing, 31:2910–2921, 2023.\n461\t[22] Yoshinobu Kajikawa, Woon-Seng Gan, and Sen M Kuo. Recent advances on active noise\n462\tcontrol: open issues and innovative applications. APSIPA Transactions on Signal and\n463\tInformation Processing, 1, 2012.\n464\t[23] Dongyuan Shi, Woon-seng Gan, Xiaoyi Shen, Zhengding Luo, and Junwei Ji. What is behind\n465\tthe meta-learning initialization of adaptive filter?—a naive method for accelerating convergence\n466\tof adaptive multichannel active noise control. Neural Networks, page 106145, 2024.\n467\t[24] Zhengding Luo, Dongyuan Shi, Xiaoyi Shen, Junwei Ji, and Woon-Seng Gan. Deep generative\n468\tfixed-filter active noise control. In ICASSP 2023-2023 IEEE International Conference on\n469\tAcoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), pages 1–5. IEEE, 2023.\n470\t[25] Junwei Ji, Dongyuan Shi, Zhengding Luo, Boxiang Wang, and Woon-Seng Gan. Self-boosted\n471\tweight-constrained fxlms: A robustness distributed active noise control algorithm without\n472\tinternode communication. IEEE Signal Processing Letters, pages 1–5, 2025.\n473\t[26] Xiaoyi Shen, Dongyuan Shi, Woon-Seng Gan, and Santi Peksi. Adaptive-gain algorithm on\n474\tthe fixed filters applied for active noise control headphone. Mechanical Systems and Signal\n475\tProcessing, 169:108641, 2022.\n476\t[27] Chelsea Finn, Pieter Abbeel, and Sergey Levine. Model-agnostic meta-learning for fast\n477\tadaptation of deep networks. In International conference on machine learning, pages 1126–\n478\t1135. PMLR, 2017.\n479\t[28] Luisa Zintgraf, Kyriacos Shiarli, Vitaly Kurin, Katja Hofmann, and Shimon Whiteson. Fast\n480\tcontext adaptation via meta-learning. In International Conference on Machine Learning, pages\n481\t7693–7702. PMLR, 2019.\n482\t[29] Dongyuan Shi, Woon-Seng Gan, Bhan Lam, and Kenneth Ooi. Fast adaptive active noise control\n483\tbased on modified model-agnostic meta-learning algorithm. IEEE Signal Processing Letters,\n484\t28:593–597, 2021.\n485\t[30] Xiaoyi Shen, Dongyuan Shi, and Woon-Seng Gan. Data-driven method to accelerate\n486\tconvergence of adaptive hybrid active noise control: Two-stage model-agnostic meta-learning.\n487\tIEEE Signal Processing Letters, 32:2654–2658, 2025.\n488\t\n489\t11|11\n490\t\n491\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "df620ffa-c420-4316-8bcf-8fedf1978a2f", "uuid": "e76fb62e-3741-4eda-9b64-2b7bcf141c10", "timestamp": "2026-08-03T07:23:36.460Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2607.29117.txt", "content": "Model-Agnostic Meta-Learning Initialization for Distributed\nMultichannel Active Noise Control\n\nXiaoyi Shen1\n1\n\narXiv:2607.29117v1 [eess.AS] 31 Jul 2026\n\nState Key Laboratory of Acoustics and Marine Information, Institute of Acoustics, Chinese Academy of Sciences,\nBeijing, 100190, China\n\nemail: shenxiaoyi@mail.ioa.ac.cn\nJunwei Ji2 , Woon-Seng Gan2\n2\n\nSchool of Electrical and Electronic Engineer, Nanyang Technological University, 639798, Singapore\n\nDongyuan Shi3\n3\n\nCenter of Intelligent Acoustics and Immersive Communications, Northwestern Polytechnical University, Xi’an, 710072,\nChina\n\nJun Yang1,4\n4\n\nSchool of Electronic, Electrical and Communication Engineering, University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing,\n100049, China\n\nABSTRACT\nDistributed multichannel active noise control (DMCANC) has emerged as a scalable framework for\nlarge-area noise reduction, where multiple nodes operate local single-channel ANC controllers and\nexchange essential information to achieve global control. A key limitation of existing DMCANC\nimplementations lies in their reliance on zero or random initialization, which leads to slow\nconvergence of adaptive filters and restricts the efficiency of inter-node collaboration. To address this\nissue, this paper introduces a model-agnostic meta-learning (MAML)–based initialization strategy\nfor DMCANC. By aggregating heterogeneous acoustic characteristics across nodes—including\nprimary and secondary paths—a MAML framework is trained to learn an initialization that\ngeneralizes effectively across distributed ANC systems. The MAML initialization is then deployed to\nall nodes to improve convergence speed under both stationary and time-varying noise conditions.\nNumerical simulations applied on broadband and real-world noise demonstrate that the proposed\nalgorithms achieves substantially faster convergence and improved noise reduction performance\ncompared with conventional DMCANC, highlighting the potential of MAML initialization as an\neffective method for large-scale ANC.\nKeywords: Active noise control (ANC), Distributed multichannel ANC, model-agnostic meta-learning\n(MAML)\n\n\fICSV32, Annual Congress of International Institute of Acoustics and Vibration (IIAV), 5 - 10 July 2026\n\n1.\n\nINTRODUCTION\n\nActive noise control (ANC) has been widely used as an effective technique for attenuating lowfrequency noise by generating anti-noise signals with the same amplitude and opposite phase [1–3].\nBenefiting from adaptive filtering algorithms such as the filtered-x least mean square (FxLMS) [4–6],\nANC systems are capable of operating in dynamic environments. With the increasing demand for\nlarge-area noise reduction, multichannel ANC (MCANC) systems have been extensively studied [7,\n8]. Conventional MCANC systems are typically implemented in a centralized manner, where a single\ncontroller processes all sensor and actuator signals [9, 10]. Although such centralized schemes can\nachieve optimal performance, they suffer from high computational complexity and limited scalability.\nTo address these issues, distributed multichannel ANC (DMCANC) systems have been proposed [11,\n12], in which multiple nodes independently perform local adaptation while exchanging information to\nachieve global noise reduction. This distributed architecture provides improved scalability, flexibility,\nand suitability for wireless implementation [13, 14].\nRecent advances in DMCANC have focused on improving system robustness and practical\nfeasibility under non-ideal communication conditions [15–17]. The mixed-gradient FxLMS\n(MGDFxLMS) algorithm applied compensation filters to combine the received gradients and\nmitigate crosstalk effects between each node [18]. MGDFxLMS has been proved haveing equivalent\nnoise reduction performance to the centralized ANC system under ideal communication conditions.\nFurthermore, intermittent communication strategies have been introduced to DMCANC (ICDMCANC) to reduce communication burden and computational complexity, while maintaining\nperformance close to centralized systems [19]. In such frameworks, each node typically runs a local\nadaptive algorithm, and global coordination is achieved through periodic information exchange.\nDespite these improvements, most existing DMCANC methods rely on zero or random initialization\nof control filters [20, 21].\nHowever, the initialization of adaptive filters plays a critical role in determining the convergence\nbehavior of ANC systems [22–24]. In distributed settings, poor initialization not only slows down\nlocal adaptation but also limits the effectiveness of inter-node collaboration, especially under\ntime-varying noise conditions [25]. This issue becomes more pronounced in large-scale DMCANC\nsystems, where heterogeneous acoustic paths and dynamic environments further complicate the\nconvergence process [26].\nTo address this limitation, we propose a model-agnostic meta-learning (MAML)-based\ninitialization framework for IC-DMCANC [27–30]. Unlike conventional approaches that treat\neach ANC node independently, the proposed method leverages meta-learning to extract shared\nknowledge from heterogeneous acoustic environments, including variations in primary and\nsecondary paths. By training across multiple tasks, the MAML framework learns a generalized\ninitialization that enables rapid adaptation when deployed in unseen DMCANC scenarios. The\nproposed method is designed to be compatible with existing distributed ANC architectures. The\nlearned initialization can be directly applied to each node as the initial control filter of the adaptive\nalgorithms, thereby significantly accelerating convergence without altering the underlying control\nstructure. Extensive simulations under broadband and real-recorded noise conditions demonstrate\nthat the proposed approach achieves faster convergence and improved noise reduction performance\ncompared with conventional DMCANC methods.\nThe remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section 2 introduces the DMCANC system\nand reviews relevant adaptive algorithms and presents the proposed MAML-based initialization\nframework. Section 3 provides performance analysis, followed by numerical simulations. Finally,\nconclusions are drawn in Section 4 .\n\n2|11\n\n\fICSV32, Annual Congress of International Institute of Acoustics and Vibration (IIAV), 5 - 10 July 2026\n\nFigure 1: The block diagram of implemetation for IC-DMCANC.\n2.\n\nPROPOSED METHOD\n\nIn order to imrove the convergence speed of IC-DMCANC, MAML is applied to pretrain the\noptimal initial control filter for each node of IC-DMCANC, which is shown in Fig.1.\n2.1. Distributed Multichannel ANC with Intermittent Communication\nDMCANC is composed of K nodes, in the k-th node, the control signal can be calculated as:\nyk (n) = wTk (n)x(n),\n\nk = 1, 2, ..., K,\n\n(1)\n\nwhere yk (n) denotes the control signal of the k-th node, and x(n) is the reference signal captured by\nthe reference sensor. wk (n) = [wk,1 (n), wk,2 (n), · · · , wk,L (n)]T represents the weights of adaptive filter\nin the k-th node with the length of L, which is updated by steepest gradient descent method:\n\u0002\n\u0003\nek − wk (n) .\nwk (n + 1) = wk (n) + µk x′kk (n)ek (n) + µk α w\n(2)\nwhere µk and α denote the step size and penalty factor for the weight update equation of k-th node.\nek stands for the center point of the range to which the control filter is restricted. ek (n) is the error\nw\nsignal of the k-th node and x′kk (n) is the filtered reference signal calculated by\nx′kk (n) = ŝkk (n) ∗ x(n),\n\n(3)\n\nwhere ŝkk (n) denotes the estimated self-secondary path skk (n) with length L s . The compensation filters\nare introduced to make up for the difference between the node’s self-secondary path and inter-node\n3|11\n\n\fICSV32, Annual Congress of International Institute of Acoustics and Vibration (IIAV), 5 - 10 July 2026\n\ncross-secondary path. The compensation filters cmk (n) can be estimated offline prior to the operation\nof the ANC system:\ncmk (n + 1) = cmk (n) + µc v′ (n)em (n),\n(4)\nwhere v′ (n) denotes to the filtered WGN vector and em (n) is given by\nem (n) = v′k (n) − v′m (n),\nand v′k (n) and v′m (n) is generated by\n\n\n\nv′k (n) =\n\n\nv′m (n) =\n\n(5)\n\nv(n) ∗ smk (n),\ncTmk (n)v(n) ∗ smm (n),\n\n(6)\n\nin which v(n) is white Gaussian noise and smk (n) is cross-secondary path.\nIntermittent communication (IC) strategy exchange data only at non-continuous, scheduled\nintervals rather than at every sampling point:\nwk (n + 1) = wk (n − N + 1) +\n\nN−1\nX\n\nµk x′kk (n − t)ek (n − t) +\n\nN−1\nX\n\n\u0002\n\u0003\nek − wk (n − t) ,\nµk α w\n\n(7)\n\nt=0\n\nt=0\n\nwhich accumulates the algorithm’s gradient information over the N steps. The new central control\nfilter can be approximated as\ne(r+1)\ne(r)\nw\n≈w\nk\nk + ϕk (n) +\n\nK\nX\n\nϕm (n) ∗ cmk (n),\n\n(8)\n\nm=1\nm,k\n\nwhere\ne(r)\nϕk (n) = wk (n + 1) − w\nk .\n\n(9)\n\n2.2. Model-Agnostic Meta-Learning Initialization\nIn order to accelerate the noise reduction process, we applied Model-Agnostic Meta-Learning\n(MAML) initialization to obtain the optimal initial coefficients wok for k-th ANC node. The dataset\nis collected seperately using primary path and seconadry path for this node. Here we use one\nnode as example. The dataset used for k-th node coefficient initialization consists of randomly\n(2)\nsampled disturbance signals {d(1)\nk (n), dk (n), · · · } and their corresponding filtered reference signal\ntracks {x′kk (1) (n), x′kk (2) (n), · · · } in different ANC configuration settings. The error signal of the j-th\nwithin-task training e⋆k ( j) (n) is obtained from\ne⋆k (n) = dk( j) (n) − [x′ (kkj) ]T (n)ϱ(kj) ,\n( j)\n\n(10)\n\nwhere ϱ(kj) represents the j-th initial value of the control filter. It is presumed that the best initial\ncontrol filter requires only a single update to reach the optimal solution wok :\nwok ( j) = ϱ(kj) − βe⋆k (n)x′kk ( j) (n),\n( j)\n\nin which β denotes the step size for the updation process in the within-task training.\nIn j-th within-task testing, the testing data pair is given by\n ( j)\n†\n\n\n= dk ( j) (n),\ndk (m)\nh\ni\n\n\nx† ( j) (m − i) = x′ ( j) (n − i) x′ ( j) (n − i − 1) · · · 01×i T ,\nkk\n\nkk\n\n(11)\n\n(12)\n\nkk\n\n4|11\n\n\fICSV32, Annual Congress of International Institute of Acoustics and Vibration (IIAV), 5 - 10 July 2026\n( j)\n\nwhere i = 1, 2, · · · , L, and the j-th within-task testing error e†k (m) is obtained from\n( j)\n\n( j)\n\n( j)\n\ne†k (m − i) = dk† (m − i) − [x†kk (m − i)]T wok ( j) .\n\n(13)\n\nAccording to the gradient descent method, the recursive formula ϱk is derived as\nϱ(kj+1) = ϱ(kj) + ϵ\n\nL−1\nX\n\n( j)\n\n( j)\n\nλi e†k (m − i)x†kk (m − i),\n\n(14)\n\ni=0\n\nwhere λ ∈ [0, 1] stands for the forgetting factor and ϵ ∈ (0, 1) denotes the learning rate. After withintask training and testing, the optimal initial value wok obtained in Equation 11 is applued for k-th node\nas the initial coefficients to further update wk (n) .\n3.\n\nSIMULATION RESULTS\n\nSimulations were conducted to validate the noise reduction performance of the proposed MAMLICDMCANC framework, implemented with six nodes as shown in Fig. 1. The primary and secondary\npaths were measured in a real acoustic environment using an acoustic chamber. The impulse response\nof the secondary path for ANC node 1 is illustrated in Fig. 2. The lengths of the secondary path,\ncompensation filter, and control filter were set to 256, 33, and 512 taps, respectively. The sampling\nfrequency for all algorithms was set at 16 kHz. For comparison, centralized implementations and\nMGDFxLMS, as well as the IC-DMCANC algorithm, were also evaluated.\nIn the MAML initialization stage, broadband noise signals with frequency ranges of 100–1200 Hz,\n800–1500 Hz, and 1200–2000 Hz were used to train the optimal initial coefficients. The training data\npairs were generated by passing the signals through the primary and secondary paths. The dataset\nwas split into 70% for training and 30% for validation. The learned initialization was then applied to\nthe adaptive filters under different noise conditions, while it was initialized as a zero vector for other\ncompared algorithms.\n3.1. Tonal noise cancellation\nA tonal noise consisting of 315 Hz and 500 Hz components was used for evaluation. The step size\nfor all algorithms was set to 1 × 10−8 . The mean square error (MSE) curves of the six nodes are shown\nin Fig. 3. It can be observed that the proposed method achieves the fastest convergence across all\nnodes. IC-DMCANC and centralized exhibit comparable convergence speed, both outperforming\nMGDFxLMS.The superior convergence performance of the proposed method is attributed to the\nMAML-based initialization, which captures prior acoustic characteristics before online adaptation.\nDue to differences in local acoustic environments, the steady-state performance varies slightly across\nnodes; however, consistent convergence acceleration is observed.\nFig. 4 presents the MSE and power spectrum of the error signals. The power spectrum was\ncomputed over the final 3 seconds of the control process. As shown in Fig. 4(b), all methods\nachieve comparable steady-state attenuation, while Fig. 4(a) demonstrates the faster convergence of\nthe proposed method.\n3.2. Broadband noise cancellation\nBroadband noise with the frequency range of 200–800 Hz was used for evaluation. The step size\nwas set to 3 × 10−7 for all algorithms. The MSE curves for each node are shown in Fig. 5. Similar to\nthe tonal noise case, the proposed method achieves the fastest convergence, while the other methods\nexhibit comparable convergence speed and similar steady-state performance.\nFig. 6 shows the convergence behavior and power spectrum for ANC node 1. All algorithms\neffectively attenuate noise within the target frequency band.\n5|11\n\n\fICSV32, Annual Congress of International Institute of Acoustics and Vibration (IIAV), 5 - 10 July 2026\n\nFigure 2: Impluse response of estimated secondary path for ANC node 1.\n\nFigure 3: Mean square error of different algorithms for tonal noise with each ANC node: (a). Mean\nsquare error in ANC node 1. (b). Mean square error in ANC node 2. (c). Mean square error in ANC\nnode 3. (d). Mean square error in ANC node 4. (e). Mean square error in ANC node 5. (f). Mean\nsquare error in ANC node 6.\n3.3. Real noise cancellation\nIn this simulation, a real recorded compressor noise was used. The step size settings were\nconsistent with the previous simulations. The results indicate that the proposed method achieves the\n6|11\n\n\fICSV32, Annual Congress of International Institute of Acoustics and Vibration (IIAV), 5 - 10 July 2026\n\nFigure 4: Convergence performance of different algorithms for tonal noise with ANC node 1:\n(a). Mean square error of different algorithms. (b). Power spectrum of error signals of different\nalgorithms.\n\nFigure 5: Mean square error of different algorithms for braobdand noise (200 − 800Hz) with each\nANC node: (a). Mean square error in ANC node 1. (b). Mean square error in ANC node 2. (c). Mean\nsquare error in ANC node 3. (d). Mean square error in ANC node 4. (e). Mean square error in ANC\nnode 5. (f). Mean square error in ANC node 6.\nfastest convergence among all the compared methods.\nFig. 7 and Fig. 8 illustrate the MSE and power spectrum of the error signals, respectively. As\nshown in Fig. 7, the proposed method suppresses the noise from the early stage of the control process,\nwhereas other methods begin to achieve noticeable attenuation after approximately 10 seconds. Fig. 8\n7|11\n\n\fICSV32, Annual Congress of International Institute of Acoustics and Vibration (IIAV), 5 - 10 July 2026\n\nFigure 6: Convergence performance of different algorithms for braobdand noise (200 − 800Hz) with\nANC node 1: (a). Mean square error of different algorithms. (b). Power spectrum of error signals of\ndifferent algorithms.\nfurther confirms that the proposed method effectively attenuates noise within the target frequency\nrange.\n\nFigure 7: Mean square error of different algorithms for real recorded noise with each ANC node: (a).\nMean square error in ANC node 1. (b). Mean square error in ANC node 2. (c). Mean square error\nin ANC node 3. (d). Mean square error in ANC node 4. (e). Mean square error in ANC node 5. (f).\nMean square error in ANC node 6.\n\n8|11\n\n\fICSV32, Annual Congress of International Institute of Acoustics and Vibration (IIAV), 5 - 10 July 2026\n\nFigure 8: Convergence performance of different algorithms real recorded noise with ANC node 1:\n(a). Mean square error of different algorithms. (b). Power spectrum of error signals of different\nalgorithms.\n4.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nThis paper proposed a model-agnostic meta-learning (MAML)-based initialization framework for\ndistributed multichannel active noise control with intermittent communication (IC-DMCANC). By\nleveraging heterogeneous acoustic information across different nodes and environments, the proposed\nmethod learned a generalized initialization that significantly improved the convergence behavior of\nadaptive control filters. Unlike conventional DMCANC approaches that relied on zero or random\ninitializations, the proposed framework enabled each node to start from a more informative initial\npoint, thereby accelerating adaptation and enhancing inter-node collaboration. The method was\nfully compatible with existing distributed ANC architectures and could be seamlessly integrated into\npractical systems without modifying the underlying control structure. Simulations under different\nnoise conditions demonstrated that the proposed method achieved faster convergence performance\ncompared with conventional methods. These findings highlighted the importance of initialization\nin DMCANC and validated the effectiveness of MAML in addressing this previously overlooked\nproblem.\nACKNOWLEDGEMENTS\nThis research is supported by the Chinese Academy of Sciences Talent Introduction Program for\nYoung Researchers (Grant Number: 552025000180).\nREFERENCES\n[1] Sen M Kuo and Dennis R Morgan. Active noise control: a tutorial review. Proceedings of the\nIEEE, 87(6):943–973, 1999.\n[2] Colin N Hansen. Understanding active noise cancellation. CRC Press, 1999.\n\n9|11\n\n\fICSV32, Annual Congress of International Institute of Acoustics and Vibration (IIAV), 5 - 10 July 2026\n\n[3] S. J. Elliott. Signal processing for active control. Academic, San Diego, Calif., 2001. Includes\nbibliographical references and index.\n[4] Simon S Haykin. Adaptive filter theory. Pearson Education India, 2002.\n[5] Chaung Shi and Yoshinobu Kajikawa. A partial-update minimax algorithm for practical\nimplementation of multi-channel feedforward active noise control. In 2018 16th International\nWorkshop on Acoustic Signal Enhancement (IWAENC), pages 1–15. IEEE, 2018.\n[6] Bhan Lam, Chuang Shi, Dongyuan Shi, and Woon-Seng Gan. Active control of sound through\nfull-sized open windows. Building and Environment, 141:16–27, 2018.\n[7] Cheng-Yuan Chang, Xiu-Wei Liu, Sen M Kuo, et al. Active noise control for centrifugal and\naxial fans. Noise Control Engineering Journal, 68(6):490–500, 2020.\n[8] Xiaoyi Shen, Yu Guo, Junwei Ji, Dongyuan Shi, and Woon-Seng Gan. Multi-channel adjoint\nleast mean square algorithm with momentum factor applied on active noise control. Mechanical\nSystems and Signal Processing, 228:112415, 2025.\n[9] Xiaoyi Shen, Dongyuan Shi, Zhengding Luo, Junwei Ji, and Woon-Seng Gan. A momentum\ntwo-gradient direction algorithm with variable step size applied to solve practical output\nconstraint issue for active noise control. In ICASSP 2023-2023 IEEE International Conference\non Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), pages 1–5. IEEE, 2023.\n[10] Woon-Seng Gan, Dongyuan Shi, and Xiaoyi Shen. Practical active noise control: Restriction of\nmaximum output power. arXiv preprint arXiv:2307.10913, 2023.\n[11] Junwei Ji, Dongyuan Shi, Zhengding Luo, Xiaoyi Shen, and Woon-Seng Gan. A practical\ndistributed active noise control algorithm overcoming communication restrictions. In 2023 Proc.\n- ICASSP IEEE Int. Conf. Acoust. Speech Signal Process., pages 1–5. IEEE, 2023.\n[12] Tianyou Li, Siyuan Lian, Sipei Zhao, Jing Lu, and Ian S. Burnett. Distributed active noise\ncontrol based on an augmented diffusion fxlms algorithm. IEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio,\nSpeech, and Language Processing, pages 1–15, 2023.\n[13] Xiaoyi Shen, Woon-Seng Gan, and Dongyuan Shi. Multi-channel wireless hybrid active noise\ncontrol with fixed-adaptive control selection. Journal of Sound and Vibration, 541:117300,\n2022.\n[14] Xiaoyi Shen, Dongyuan Shi, Zhengding Luo, Junwei Ji, and Woon-Seng Gan. A survey\nof integrating wireless technology into active noise control. In INTER-NOISE and NOISECON Congress and Conference Proceedings, volume 270, pages 1624–1637. Institute of Noise\nControl Engineering, 2024.\n[15] Y. J. Chu, C. M. Mak, Y. Zhao, S. C. Chan, and M. Wu. Performance analysis of a diffusion\ncontrol method for anc systems and the network design. Journal of Sound and Vibration,\n475:115273, 2020.\n[16] Tianyou Li, Li Rao, Sipei Zhao, Hongji Duan, Jing Lu, and Ian S. Burnett. An augmented\ndiffusion algorithm with bidirectional communication for a distributed active noise control\nsystem. The Journal of the Acoustical Society of America, 154(6):3568–3579, 2023.\n[17] Yang Zhou, Haiquan Zhao, Dongxu Liu, and Xinnian Guo. Distributed active noise control\nrobust to impulsive interference. IEEE Sensors Journal, 2025.\n[18] Junwei Ji, Dongyuan Shi, and Woon-Seng Gan. Mixed-gradients distributed filtered reference\nleast mean square algorithm – a robust distributed multichannel active noise control algorithm.\nIEEE Transactions on Audio, Speech and Language Processing, 33:1563–1575, 2025.\n[19] Junwei Ji, Dongyuan Shi, Xiaoyi Shen, Woon-Seng Gan, Jie Chen, and Jun Yang.\nImplementation of distributed multichannel active noise control with intermittent\ncommunication and coprocessor assisted data combination.\nMechanical Systems and\nSignal Processing, 248:114024, 2026.\n10|11\n\n\fICSV32, Annual Congress of International Institute of Acoustics and Vibration (IIAV), 5 - 10 July 2026\n\n[20] Dongyuan Shi, Bhan Lam, Kenneth Ooi, Xiaoyi Shen, and Woon-Seng Gan. Selective\nfixed-filter active noise control based on convolutional neural network. Signal Processing,\n190:108317, 2022.\n[21] Dongyuan Shi, Woon-Seng Gan, Bhan Lam, Zhengding Luo, and Xiaoyi Shen. Transferable\nlatent of cnn-based selective fixed-filter active noise control. IEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio,\nSpeech, and Language Processing, 31:2910–2921, 2023.\n[22] Yoshinobu Kajikawa, Woon-Seng Gan, and Sen M Kuo. Recent advances on active noise\ncontrol: open issues and innovative applications. APSIPA Transactions on Signal and\nInformation Processing, 1, 2012.\n[23] Dongyuan Shi, Woon-seng Gan, Xiaoyi Shen, Zhengding Luo, and Junwei Ji. What is behind\nthe meta-learning initialization of adaptive filter?—a naive method for accelerating convergence\nof adaptive multichannel active noise control. Neural Networks, page 106145, 2024.\n[24] Zhengding Luo, Dongyuan Shi, Xiaoyi Shen, Junwei Ji, and Woon-Seng Gan. Deep generative\nfixed-filter active noise control. In ICASSP 2023-2023 IEEE International Conference on\nAcoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), pages 1–5. IEEE, 2023.\n[25] Junwei Ji, Dongyuan Shi, Zhengding Luo, Boxiang Wang, and Woon-Seng Gan. Self-boosted\nweight-constrained fxlms: A robustness distributed active noise control algorithm without\ninternode communication. IEEE Signal Processing Letters, pages 1–5, 2025.\n[26] Xiaoyi Shen, Dongyuan Shi, Woon-Seng Gan, and Santi Peksi. Adaptive-gain algorithm on\nthe fixed filters applied for active noise control headphone. Mechanical Systems and Signal\nProcessing, 169:108641, 2022.\n[27] Chelsea Finn, Pieter Abbeel, and Sergey Levine. Model-agnostic meta-learning for fast\nadaptation of deep networks. In International conference on machine learning, pages 1126–\n1135. PMLR, 2017.\n[28] Luisa Zintgraf, Kyriacos Shiarli, Vitaly Kurin, Katja Hofmann, and Shimon Whiteson. Fast\ncontext adaptation via meta-learning. In International Conference on Machine Learning, pages\n7693–7702. PMLR, 2019.\n[29] Dongyuan Shi, Woon-Seng Gan, Bhan Lam, and Kenneth Ooi. Fast adaptive active noise control\nbased on modified model-agnostic meta-learning algorithm. IEEE Signal Processing Letters,\n28:593–597, 2021.\n[30] Xiaoyi Shen, Dongyuan Shi, and Woon-Seng Gan. Data-driven method to accelerate\nconvergence of adaptive hybrid active noise control: Two-stage model-agnostic meta-learning.\nIEEE Signal Processing Letters, 32:2654–2658, 2025.\n\n11|11\n\n\f", "numLines": 491, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 491}}}
